WEBVTT

00:01.680 --> 00:06.480
Le Space escrow01. Confidential payments by passkey.

00:07.610 --> 00:16.410
Alice hands a task to Bob and locks its budget in escrow. A demo on Ethereum’s Sepolia testnet: no real money.

00:16.626 --> 00:23.906
Alice wants to hand a task to Bob and pay him once it is done. How much she pays should stay between them.

00:24.439 --> 00:30.759
The start dialog says what this is: confidential payments by passkey. One button.

00:31.316 --> 00:35.716
Alice creates a passkey. No password, no seed phrase.

00:36.322 --> 00:43.602
A first passkey takes three confirmations. Before the second and the third, the app says what each one is for.

00:44.077 --> 00:46.557
Bob does the same in his own browser.

00:58.948 --> 01:09.348
Behind each passkey is an account on Sepolia: Uniswap’s Calibur smart account, through EIP-7702. Openfort pays the gas.

01:12.517 --> 01:21.237
Alice starts in a private list of her own, made with her first task. It is sealed: only she can read it and write to it.

01:21.615 --> 01:24.575
She adds a task and delegates it to Bob.

01:27.601 --> 01:36.561
And a budget: 500 cUSDT, Zama’s confidential token. The amount is encrypted in her browser before it leaves.

01:36.932 --> 01:40.132
One passkey confirmation locks it in escrow.

01:42.520 --> 01:51.400
Zama’s encryption hides the amount, not the parties. The chain shows who locked something for whom, and when. Never how much.

01:51.881 --> 02:03.321
The task is sealed too. The relay that keeps the list, and anyone with its address, see only an envelope. Bob gets the key to this one task, and to nothing else.

02:04.211 --> 02:06.851
Alice shares the list’s address with Bob.

02:15.646 --> 02:23.886
Bob opens it. The light under the list name turns green: their two browsers are now connected directly, peer to peer.

02:24.365 --> 02:30.045
The task is delegated to Bob: he reads it, and the budget, on his own device.

02:30.423 --> 02:33.783
He ticks it off and signs the change with his passkey.

02:34.270 --> 02:38.350
Alice sees it at once. The budget waits for her release.

02:39.405 --> 02:44.205
Alice releases the budget. Only she can, and only once.

02:50.739 --> 02:59.059
Bob’s confidential balance now holds the 500. It is decrypted on his device; on the chain it stays encrypted.

03:02.818 --> 03:10.178
The escrow contract names one auditor. The auditor can read every amount, but cannot move any money.

03:12.566 --> 03:20.486
This view lists the escrows on the chain. Their amounts open only with the auditor’s own key, so here they stay hidden.

03:21.466 --> 03:28.026
One passkey for identity, data and payment. The task and the amount stay confidential.

03:31.208 --> 03:43.768
escrow01 by Le Space. Try it at escrow01 dot le-space dot de. Building passkey wallets, confidential payments or local-first apps? Let’s talk.
